        "text": "100"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "113"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "What does one turn do?",
        "body": "Try it with 10 marbles: remove half → 5, add 2 → 7, double → 14. With 20: 10, 12, 24. Each turn simply **adds 4** marbles: half of the marbles is taken away, but doubling brings it back, and the 2 added marbles become 4.\n\nAfter 35 turns George has $35\\times 4 = 140$ more marbles than at the start: $148 - 140 = 8$.\n\n**Answer: (A) 8**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Work backwards",
        "body": "Undo one turn: halve, take away 2, double. From 148: 74, 72, 144 — so one turn backwards takes away 4. Going back 35 turns: $148 - 35\\times4 = 148 - 140 = 8$. Check: starting with 8, the first turn gives 4, 6, 12 — indeed 4 more.\n\n**Answer: (A) 8**"
      }
